    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of a point on a line that passes through two points, we can use the direction vector of the line and the coordinates of one of the points.\n\n1. **Find the direction vector:**\n   The direction vector \\(\\vec{d}\\) can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n   \\[\n   \\vec{d} = (5, 1, -2) - (2, 2, 1) = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Use the direction vector to find the relationship between the point \\( P \\), the given point \\( Q \\), and the parameters:**\n   Let \\( P \\) be a point on the line with coordinates \\( (x, y, z) \\) and \\( Q \\) be one of the points through which the line passes with coordinates \\( (2, 2, 1) \\).\n   The coordinates of \\( P \\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   (x, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + t \\vec{d}\n   \\]\n   Given that \\( t \\) is the direction parameter and \\( t = 2 \\) (since \\( x = 2 \\)), we have:\n   \\[\n   P = (2, 2, 1) + 2 \\cdot (3, -1, -3)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the coordinates of \\( P \\):**\n   If the coordinates of \\( P \\) are given to be \\( (4, y, z) \\) with the \\( x \\)-coordinate \\( x = 4 \\), then this directly gives \\( x = 4 \\).\n   So, we calculate the coordinates:\n   \\[\n   (4, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + 2 \\cdot (3, -1, -3)\n   \\]\n   Perform the scalar multiplication and vector addition:\n   \\[\n   (4, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + (6, -2, -6)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (4, y, z) = (2 + 6, 2 - 2, 1 - 6)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (4, y, z) = (8, 0, -5)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Identify the \\( z \\)-coordinate:**\n   From the calculation above, the \\( z \\)-coordinate is:\n   \\[\n   z = -5\n   \\]\n\nThus, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is \\boxed{-5}.",
