      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to find the equation of the line passing through the points $(2, 2, 1)$ and $(5, 1, -2)$.\n\nThe direction vector of the line can be calculated as follows:\n\n$\\vec{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)$\n\nExpress this direction vector in a matrix vector notation:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can represent the line in parametric form as follows:\n\n$x = 2 + 3t$\n$y = 2 - t$\n$z = 1 - 3t$\n\nWe're given that the line passes through a point $P$ whose coordinates are proportional to $x = 4$.\n\nTo express the coordinates of $P$, we substitute $x = 4$ into the equation obtaining $t$:\n\n$t = \\frac{4 - 2}{-3} = \\frac{2}{-3} = -\\frac{2}{3}$ \n\nNow we substitute $t$ into the equation for $z$:\n\n$z = 1 - 3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 + 2$\n\nHence, the $z$-coordinate of the point $P$ is:\n\n$\\boxed{3}$",
